language ESSENCE' 1.0
$ tsp: an Essence' model from DCP Rosetta.
$
$ The travelling salesman problem, also known as the travelling salesperson problem (TSP), asks the following
$ question: "Given a list of cities and the distances between each pair of cities (or the locations of the cities),
$ what is the shortest possible route that visits each city exactly once and returns to the origin city?" The
$ distance is defined as the Euclidean distance between the points rounded to an integer.
$
$ Save this file as model.eprime. Download `tsp.json` and `dcp_convert_essence.py` from the
$ problem's page:
$   https://dcp-bench.github.io/DCP-Rosetta/problems/tsp.html
$ Convert the instances once, then run the first one with Savile Row 1.11 (it needs Java); the
$ solution is written to `tsp_1.param.solution`:
$   python dcp_convert_essence.py tsp.json
$   savilerow model.eprime tsp_1.param -run-solver

$ locations_dim1: the number of cities (the length of the locations list, derived from the data)
given locations_dim1 : int
$ locations[c, 1], locations[c, 2]: the x and y coordinates of city c (indices 1-based)
given locations : matrix indexed by [int(1..locations_dim1), int(1..2)] of int

$ n: the number of cities
letting n = locations_dim1
$ sumsq: the sum over all cities of the squared coordinates; no distance exceeds twice this value
letting sumsq = sum c : int(1..n) . (locations[c, 1] * locations[c, 1] + locations[c, 2] * locations[c, 2])

$ dist[i, j]: the Euclidean distance between cities i and j, rounded to an integer
find dist : matrix indexed by [int(1..n), int(1..n)] of int(0..2 * sumsq)
$ succ[i]: the city visited right after city i on the route
find succ : matrix indexed by [int(1..n)] of int(1..n)
$ pos[i]: the position of city i on the route, city 1 at position 1 (used to rule out subtours)
find pos : matrix indexed by [int(1..n)] of int(1..n)
$ travel_distance: the length of the route, the sum of the distances of its legs
find travel_distance : int(0..n * 2 * sumsq)

minimising travel_distance
such that
  $ the rounded distance between two cities is the Euclidean distance rounded to an integer
  forAll i : int(1..n) . forAll j : int(1..n) .
    ((locations[i, 1] - locations[j, 1]) * (locations[i, 1] - locations[j, 1])
       + (locations[i, 2] - locations[j, 2]) * (locations[i, 2] - locations[j, 2]) = 0 /\ dist[i, j] = 0)
    \/ ((2 * dist[i, j] - 1) * (2 * dist[i, j] - 1)
          <= 4 * ((locations[i, 1] - locations[j, 1]) * (locations[i, 1] - locations[j, 1])
                  + (locations[i, 2] - locations[j, 2]) * (locations[i, 2] - locations[j, 2]))
        /\ 4 * ((locations[i, 1] - locations[j, 1]) * (locations[i, 1] - locations[j, 1])
                  + (locations[i, 2] - locations[j, 2]) * (locations[i, 2] - locations[j, 2]))
          < (2 * dist[i, j] + 1) * (2 * dist[i, j] + 1)),
  $ every city is visited exactly once: the successors are all different
  allDiff(succ),
  $ no city is its own successor
  forAll i : int(1..n) . succ[i] != i,
  $ city 1 is at position 1 of the route
  pos[1] = 1,
  $ the route is one single circuit through all cities: each leg that does not return to city 1 goes to the next position
  forAll i : int(1..n) . (succ[i] = 1 \/ pos[succ[i]] = pos[i] + 1),
  $ the travel distance is the sum of the distances of the legs of the route
  travel_distance = sum i : int(1..n) . dist[i, succ[i]]
